Here is a first survey. Sources are the local notmuch 0.40 man pages
and notmuchmail.org, fetched today. I mark guesses as guesses. It
follows from "Re: need some way to close the loop on threads" [1],
where notmuch-git(1) came up as the next step if one shared archive
dir stops scaling.

The short version: the notmuch project tracks patch state as shared
tags in a git repo (nmbug, now notmuch-git). Its tags are judgments
by people, not facts that a hook can derive. Personal setups are
mostly a "new" tag plus a post-new hook, with sync tools on top.

notmuch-git(1)
--------------

- It commits tags that match a prefix from the notmuch database into
  a bare git repo, and checks them back out. Subcommands: init,
  clone, commit, checkout, fetch, merge, pull, push, status, log,
  archive. [man notmuch-git]
- It started as nmbug, a devel/ tool for the project's own patch
  tracker. 0.37 (2022-08) promoted it to notmuch-git. Invoked as
  nmbug (or with -N), it keeps the old defaults: repo at ~/.nmbug and
  prefix notmuch::. Otherwise the defaults are
  $XDG_DATA_HOME/notmuch/<profile>/git and no prefix, i.e. all tags.
  git.path and git.tag_prefix set them in config.
- Storage is one empty file per tag, tags/<msgid>/<tag>. Format 1
  adds hashed directory levels. So the repo knows only Message-IDs
  and tag names, not mail.
- Tags for a message you do not have yet are kept (status "U"), so
  tags can arrive before the mail does.
- pull is a plain git merge, and it refuses while you have
  uncommitted tag changes. My guess, not verified: since each tag is
  its own empty file, real conflicts are rare.
- git.safe_fraction (default 0.1) makes commit and checkout refuse
  to change more than that share of messages without -f. That
  protects against a runaway script.
- muchsync is the other common sync tool. It syncs mail files and
  all tags between your own machines. notmuch-git syncs only tags,
  only a prefix, through any git remote, with history and several
  writers. muchsync is for one person; notmuch-git is for a group.

How the notmuch list uses it together
-------------------------------------

- The tags all carry the notmuch:: prefix. The core set from the
  nmbug page: patch, wip, needs-review, trivial, moreinfo ("waiting
  for feedback from patch proposer or others"), obsolete ("replaced
  by some other patch"), stale ("no longer applies to master"),
  pushed ("is pushed to master"), wontfix, bug, fixed. There are
  also topic tags (emacs, doc, test, python, ...) and release tags.
- Setup: nmbug clone https://nmbug.notmuchmail.org/git/nmbug-tags.git,
  then checkout. The loop is pull, tag, commit, push. Push access is
  granted by David Bremner, who asks for an ssh key, ideally in a
  gpg-signed mail. Anyone can read.
- The status page (nmbug.notmuchmail.org/status/) has views like
  Review, Moreinfo, Stale, Bugs and "Maybe ready". Each view is a
  saved notmuch query over those tags. notmuch-report (formerly
  nmbug-status) generates it from a JSON config kept on a branch of
  the same tags repo, so everyone sees the same queues.
- Triage (notmuchmail.org/contributing/): developers tag incoming
  patches. Contributors with access are asked to tag their own, and
  to tag the old version obsolete when they send -vN. A resend cites
  the previous version's Message-ID rather than threading under it.
  That is what process-email already does.
- Patches still go through git send-email and git am. nmbug plays
  the part patchwork would, but as distributed tags, not a central
  database. I found nothing saying they ever used patchwork.
- Side note: that page says LLM-generated contributions or
  interactions are not accepted, so I would not post there.

Personal usage
--------------

- new.tags=new, then a post-new hook that runs notmuch tag queries
  on tag:new and finally removes new (notmuchmail.org/initial_tagging).
  afew is a popular ready-made version of that hook. Our mail-tag.sh
  is the same pattern.
- search.exclude_tags=deleted;spam is the default from notmuch
  setup. We exclude deleted.
- Delivery with notmuch insert from procmail, sieve or an MDA,
  tagging on the way in; pre-new hooks that run the fetcher.
- Emacs: notmuch-saved-searches, address completion, msmtp or
  sendmail to send, notmuch-fcc-dirs for sent mail. Named queries
  (query.<name> in config) work from the CLI too.
- Sync across machines: muchsync, notmuch-git with a personal
  prefix, running notmuch over ssh, or lieer for Gmail labels.
- Bremner's git-remote-notmuch, used with git-annex, puts mail and
  tags in one git repo. That is the closest to how we commit our
  maildir. It was posted to the list as v3 in 2025-08. I could not
  confirm it is merged, and he warns it is experimental.

What this means for us
----------------------

You asked in [1] why they need these tools at all. This confirms
the answer there. Their list is not in git: each person gets the
mail by SMTP, so only the Message-ID is shared, and tags travel on
their own. Ours is in the tree, so derived tags like inbox, sent and
replied need no sync, because every checkout derives the same ones.

The gap is judged state, which a hook cannot rebuild: archived for
now, and later patch status (needs-review, moreinfo, obsolete,
applied). If that outgrows the archive dir, notmuch-git fits:

- Put judged tags under a prefix, e.g. lab::, and set
  git.tag_prefix to it. Hook-derived tags then never enter the
  tags repo.
- The tags repo is a separate bare git repo, so it does not touch
  the jj workspaces. It can follow the same two remotes: I push my
  tags to submissions, you merge and publish the canonical ones.
  That is nmbug's trust model too.
- Status views could live in the repo as query.<name> entries, so
  you and I see the same Review and Moreinfo queues.

For per-person state like "I have read this", a personal prefix in
a personal repo would do it, without touching the shared tree.

If you want, I can try a next step: a throwaway notmuch-git repo
with a lab:: prefix over a copy of the list, to see how it fits
with jj and the hooks before anything changes in the real setup.

